steps.body:
Every crossing has a price. A `ctypes` call converts each argument from a Python object, goes through a general-purpose calling path, and converts the result back. This Mac timed an EWMA over 10^6 mid prices three ways with `time.perf_counter`: a plain Python loop, one `quant_ewma_update` call per tick, and one `quant_ewma_update_many` call on the whole numpy array:

```text
$ python3 cost.py
pure Python loop:           17.2 ms  19276.356876
one ctypes call a tick:    198.0 ms  19276.356876
one ctypes call in all:      1.5 ms  19276.356876
```

Calling C++ once per tick was 11 times *slower* than plain Python, about 200 ns of overhead per call for under 2 ns of work. One call for the whole array was 11 times faster than Python, and all three agree on the answer. So design the boundary for batches: arrays in, arrays out, one call per backtest or per chunk, never one per tick.

During a `ctypes` call, Python releases the **GIL**, the global interpreter lock that lets only one thread run Python at a time, so other Python threads keep going while the C++ works. Python 3.13 also comes in an optional build without a GIL.

Declaring every signature by hand gets old, and a wrong one crashes. **pybind11** writes the binding in C++ instead. It's a header-only library that turns C++ functions and classes into a real Python module, takes numpy arrays, turns C++ exceptions into Python ones (`std::invalid_argument` becomes `ValueError`), and lets you release the GIL. It isn't installed on this Mac or in the sandbox, so here are the file and the commands, not their output:

```cpp norun
// quant_py.cpp: a Python module written in C++
#include <pybind11/numpy.h>
#include <pybind11/pybind11.h>
#include <stdexcept>

namespace py = pybind11;
using Array = py::array_t<double, py::array::c_style | py::array::forcecast>;

class Ewma {
public:
    explicit Ewma(double alpha) : alpha_(alpha) {
        if (!(alpha > 0.0 && alpha <= 1.0)) throw std::invalid_argument("alpha must be in (0, 1]");
    }
    void update(double x) { mean_ = count_++ == 0 ? x : mean_ + alpha_ * (x - mean_); }
    double value() const { return mean_; }

private:
    double alpha_;
    double mean_ = 0.0;
    long long count_ = 0;
};

double vwap(Array prices, Array sizes) {
    if (prices.size() != sizes.size()) throw std::invalid_argument("lengths differ");
    const double* p = prices.data();
    const double* s = sizes.data();
    const py::ssize_t n = prices.size();
    double notional = 0.0, volume = 0.0;
    {
        py::gil_scoped_release release;  // no Python objects touched in here
        for (py::ssize_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            notional += p[i] * s[i];
            volume += s[i];
        }
    }
    return notional / volume;
}

PYBIND11_MODULE(quant, m) {
    m.def("vwap", &vwap, py::arg("prices"), py::arg("sizes"));
    py::class_<Ewma>(m, "Ewma")
        .def(py::init<double>(), py::arg("alpha"))
        .def("update", &Ewma::update)
        .def_property_readonly("value", &Ewma::value);
}
```

